WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.970 --> 00:00:05.240
We will now formulate the clustering
problem that we define informally in

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:08.660
the previous section as
a rigorous computational problem.

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:14.780
And I think a good clustering should
satisfy the Good Clustering Principle,

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.940
which says that elements
within the same cluster

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:23.730
should be closer to each other than
elements in different clusters.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:28.889
Or, in other words, there should
be a parameter "delta" such that

00:00:28.889 --> 00:00:34.464
distance between elements in
the same cluster is less than delta,

00:00:34.464 --> 00:00:40.280
and distance between elements in
different clusters is larger than delta.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:44.810
For example, is this clustering
satisfying the Good Clustering Principle?

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:49.650
Of course not because there are
elements in the same cluster that

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:54.580
are further away from each other than from
elements in different clusters.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:58.390
However, this problem is easy to
fix because here's a clustering

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:00.830
satisfying the Good Clustering Principle.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:07.790
And therefore, we come up
the following Clustering Problem:

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:11.950
Find a partition of expression
vectors into clusters

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:16.580
that satisfies
the Good Clustering Principle.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.058
But how about the example below this line?

00:01:19.058 --> 00:01:24.000
Can we construct clustering satisfying
the Good Clustering Principle?

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.690
It turns out that it doesn't matter
how much we try: there is no

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:34.430
partition satisfying the Good
Clustering Principle for this dataset,

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:39.106
which means that the problem that we just
formulated, while it is a well defined

00:01:39.106 --> 00:01:43.878
computational problem, is essentially
useless for most practical dataset

00:01:43.878 --> 00:01:48.799
because most of them do not
have a partition satisfying

00:01:48.799 --> 00:01:51.680
the Good Clustering Principle.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:57.700
Thus, we have to figure out how to
construct a new objective function for

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:03.530
clustering that will generate
a solution for any dataset.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.650
Here is a small dataset of four points

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:09.710
and four different ways to cluster.

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:13.020
Which of them is the best cluster?

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:20.450
Our goal now is to come up with the notion
of the objective function for clustering.

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:25.156
And to do this, I will take
liberty to reformulate the goal of

00:02:25.156 --> 00:02:30.350
clustering into the equivalent goal
of finding centers of clusters.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:37.200
For example, below, you can see a dataset
consisting of eight blue points and

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:42.570
three points representing Centers
of clusters for this dataset.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:49.590
Our goal is to come up with the notion
of distance between Data and Centers,

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:54.086
and then after we come up with the notion
of distance between Data and Centers,

00:02:54.086 --> 00:02:58.960
we want to find Centers that
minimize this distance.

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:03.250
But what is the distance
from Data to Centers?

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:07.930
To define the notion of distance
from Data to Centers, we will first

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:12.830
define the notion of distance from
a single DataPoint to Centers.

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:16.500
And in this case,
it's natural to define this distance

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:21.260
as the distance from a DataPoint
to it's closest Center.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:26.087
For example,
the distance from this blue point will

00:03:26.087 --> 00:03:31.218
be the distance to its closest center,
and we can construct

00:03:31.218 --> 00:03:35.560
distances to all four
DataPoints in this way.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:42.220
Now, we only defined the distance
from a single DataPoint to Centers.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:47.090
Our goal now is to define the distance
from all DataPoints to Centers.

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:50.370
What will this natural
notion of such distance?

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:55.165
And I think maximum distance among
the distances we just computed would

00:03:55.165 --> 00:03:59.680
be the natural notion of the
distance between Data and Centers.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:05.490
That's how we define the MaxDistance
objective function for clustering.

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:08.561
After we define the notion of MaxDistance,

00:04:08.561 --> 00:04:12.890
we are ready to formulate
the k-Center Clustering Problem:

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:15.490
Given a set of points Data,

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:20.710
find k Centers minimizing maximum
distance between Data and Centers.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.720
You see at the bottom of the slide
an example with eight blue points and

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:27.270
three Centers.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:30.490
These three Centers are very poorly chosen

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:33.250
as a solution of the k-Center
Clustering Problem.

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:36.170
These Centers would be a better solution.

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:40.660
But is it the optimal solution of
the k-Center Clustering Problem?

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:45.070
Actually no, because there is a different
partition of points in the clusters

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:48.940
that gives an even better solution of
the k-Center Clustering Problem.

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:54.929
Anyway, it appears that the k-Center
Clustering Problem appears to be a rather

00:04:54.929 --> 00:04:59.298
simple problem, but it turns out
that it's actually NP-Complete.

00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:06.310
Therefore, we have to develop
an approximation algorithm for solving it,

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:11.400
and our next task is to create a
k-Center Clustering Heuristic

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:14.962
that is called the FarthestFirstTraversal.

00:05:14.962 --> 00:05:18.150
The FarthestFirstTraversal starts from

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:23.090
choosing an arbitrary point as
the first Center of the cluster.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:29.210
At the next step, which point
would you choose as the next Center?

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:35.550
The reasonable approach is to choose
the point that is the farthest apart

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:40.490
from the existing cluster,
which will be this point.

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:45.170
Afterwards, we find that the distances
from the already constructed clusters

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:47.520
to all DataPoints,

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:52.570
and to choose the next cluster,
we simply select the distance

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:57.860
that is largest among the constructed
distances and this is our third cluster.

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:01.820
If we only need to construct three clusters,
we are done, and this is the clustering:

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:07.500
a partitioning of eight points into
three clusters that

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.618
the FarthestFirstTraversal produces.

00:06:10.618 --> 00:06:16.330
Now, after we design
the FarthestFirstTraversal algorithm,

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:20.960
lets ask the question: What is wrong
with this algorithm in practice?

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:26.500
Well, FarthestFirstTraversal selects
Centers that minimize the maximum distance.

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:33.230
But biologists are often interested in
typical, rather than maximum, distances

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:40.460
because maximum distances often
represent experimental errors or outliers.

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:44.860
For example,
in this case of a simple clustering

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:49.230
problem that is presented below,
let's see how the human eye and

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:53.090
FarthestFirstTraversal would solve
the problem.

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:57.586
The human eye will immediately see
three clusters and will ignore outliers.

00:06:57.586 --> 00:07:02.606
FarthestFirstTraversal, on
the other hand, will always

00:07:02.606 --> 00:07:08.461
construct three clusters and miss
the correct clustering of the points.

00:07:08.461 --> 00:07:13.979
We therefore need to develop
a different approach to clustering

00:07:13.979 --> 00:07:16.836
that works well with outliers.

00:07:16.836 --> 00:07:19.802
To come up with this new
clustering approach,

00:07:19.802 --> 00:07:23.540
we first need to modify
objective function for clustering.

00:07:24.592 --> 00:07:28.860
Our MaxDistance Objective Function
designed for

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:31.820
the k-Center Clustering Problem
has its limitations.

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:38.440
Please note that only one of the distances
shown below contributes to MaxDistance.

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:41.920
Instead, we will use a new
Objective Function

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:44.340
called the squared error distortion.

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:49.770
The squared error distortion
is the sum of squares

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:54.550
of all distances, divided by the
number of points in the dataset.

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:57.630
So, for the maximum distance,

00:07:57.630 --> 00:08:01.590
a single DataPoint contributes
to the objective function,

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:07.880
while for the distortion, all DataPoints
contribute to the objective function.

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:11.110
And as soon as we define
the squared error distortion,

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:14.590
we define the k-Means Clustering Problem.

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:17.470
And the only difference between the
k-Center Clustering Problem and

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:18.570
the k-Means Clustering Problem

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:23.620
is that the k-Means clustering problem is
that the k-Means Clustering Problem tries

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:29.210
to optimize distortion,
rather than maximum distance.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:36.180
In this case, the Centers chosen shown
in this slide are not good Centers,

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:40.030
and after we apply the k-Centers
Clustering Problem,

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:42.350
they will move to different points.

00:08:42.350 --> 00:08:44.946
After we apply the k-Means
Clustering Problem,

00:08:44.946 --> 00:08:47.170
they also will move to different points,

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:51.875
and please note that they
have moved to different

00:08:51.875 --> 00:08:57.060
positions than in the k-Center
Clustering Problem.

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:01.490
Now, it may appear that k-Means
Clustering Problem is simple, but

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:06.100
it turns out to be NP-Hard even for

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:11.620
partitioning the set of DataPoints
into just two clusters.

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:15.825
The only case when
there are polynomial algorithms for

00:09:15.825 --> 00:09:21.730
the k-Means Clustering Problem is
the case when k is equal to one.

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:26.330
You may think that there is no point
in clustering DataPoints into one cluster,

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:28.570
because this clustering is given.

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:32.480
Indeed, we are not trying to
cluster the dataset into one cluster:

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:36.020
we are trying to find the best
center of this cluster and

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:40.220
you will see in the next section that
it will be actually very helpful.

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:44.760
However, how do we find
a center of a cluster?

00:09:46.336 --> 00:09:48.415
So,

00:09:48.415 --> 00:09:53.780
how do we develop an algorithm for k-Means
Clustering when k is equal to 1?

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:57.590
Well, in this case, there is

00:09:57.590 --> 00:10:02.830
the Center of Gravity Theorem, which
says that the center of gravity of

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:07.690
points Data is the only point,
remember, only is important here,

00:10:07.690 --> 00:10:12.470
the only point solving
the 1-Means Clustering Problem.

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:16.217
To remind you,
the center of gravity of points Data,

00:10:16.217 --> 00:10:19.007
if we represent every point as a vector,

00:10:19.007 --> 00:10:25.060
is simply the sum of these vectors divided
by the number of vectors in the dataset,

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:31.440
or, in other words, the i-th coordinate
of the center of gravity is simple

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:36.405
the average of the i-th coordinate
of all points in the dataset.

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:42.984
And now, after we introduce
the notion of k-means clustering,

00:10:42.984 --> 00:10:46.670
we are ready to describe
the Lloyd algorithm,

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:49.681
the most popular
approximation algorithm for

00:10:49.681 --> 00:10:52.545
solving the k-Means Clustering Problem.

